[{"paper":{"id":"2611.07624","authors":[{"_id":"6af133ba05af6789c0631ece","name":"Xiaobo Wang","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af133ba05af6789c0631ecf","name":"Zimu Wang","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af133ba05af6789c0631ed0","name":"Siyu Ye","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af133ba05af6789c0631ed1","name":"Jiaqi Li","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af133ba05af6789c0631ed2","name":"Min Tang","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af133ba05af6789c0631ed3","name":"Jun Bai","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af133ba05af6789c0631ed4","name":"Yuling Shi","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af133ba05af6789c0631ed5","name":"Ruobing Wang","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af133ba05af6789c0631ed6","name":"Jingjing Zhang","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af133ba05af6789c0631ed7","name":"Chaofan Wang","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af133ba05af6789c0631ed8","name":"Kai Wu","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af133ba05af6789c0631ed9","name":"Mengnan Qi","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af133ba05af6789c0631eda","name":"Kai Cai","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af133ba05af6789c0631edb","name":"Xiaodong Gu","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af133ba05af6789c0631edc","name":"Qi Liu","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af133ba05af6789c0631edd","name":"Zilong Zheng","hidden":false}],"publishedAt":"2026-11-05T00:00:00.000Z","submittedOnDailyAt":"2026-11-09T00:00:00.000Z","title":"Beneath the Surface: What We Overlook When We Talk About Agent Evolution","submittedOnDailyBy":{"_id":"65e933fcacb29a596cd6eb6e","avatarUrl":"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65e933fcacb29a596cd6eb6e/dlH14rO2I4EUMVyjEjdRK.jpeg","isPro":false,"fullname":"Xiaobo Wang","user":"Yofuria","type":"user","name":"Yofuria"},"summary":"Agent harness evolution enables recursive self-improvement (RSI) without updating model weights. We examine whether higher benchmark scores yield reliable, efficient, and transferable improvement. We evaluate four methods across harnesses, general and coding tasks. Repeated evaluations, controlled agent expansion, component interventions, and admission replays reveal recurring patterns. All sixteen evolved agents gain previously uncovered tasks while losing previously covered ones. In one general-task case, reliability rises by 5.2 points despite losing ten previously covered tasks. Most reliability gains also increase deployment cost. Specific changes usually outperform generic reminders, but their benefits can change when combined. The highest-scoring agent does not yield the largest mean improvement in any of our four pools, and stricter admission can discard useful alternatives. Transfer selectively preserves gains: coverage usually falls on the general-task target across both harness types and paradigms, but rises on the coding target with the outer-layer-only harness. These findings motivate preservation-aware evaluation, tested decision rules, and explicit applicability conditions. As a constructive follow-up, EvoAudit turns these diagnostics into revisions of select, analyze, mutate, and gate, achieving the best performance.","upvotes":12,"discussionId":"6af133ba05af6789c0631ede","organization":{"_id":"67ff908ff0f413c693b7cd0c","name":"ustc-community","fullname":"University of Science and Technology of China","avatar":"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/660c2d134ba2fcc848b03e21/j_f3uYYIFPH_4WJH9fKel.png"}},"publishedAt":"2026-11-04T19:00:00.000Z","title":"Beneath the Surface: What We Overlook When We Talk About Agent Evolution","summary":"Agent harness evolution enables recursive self-improvement (RSI) without updating model weights. We examine whether higher benchmark scores yield reliable, efficient, and transferable improvement. We evaluate four methods across harnesses, general and coding tasks. Repeated evaluations, controlled agent expansion, component interventions, and admission replays reveal recurring patterns. All sixteen evolved agents gain previously uncovered tasks while losing previously covered ones. In one general-task case, reliability rises by 5.2 points despite losing ten previously covered tasks. Most reliability gains also increase deployment cost. Specific changes usually outperform generic reminders, but their benefits can change when combined. The highest-scoring agent does not yield the largest mean improvement in any of our four pools, and stricter admission can discard useful alternatives. Transfer selectively preserves gains: coverage usually falls on the general-task target across both harness types and paradigms, but rises on the coding target with the outer-layer-only harness. These findings motivate preservation-aware evaluation, tested decision rules, and explicit applicability conditions. As a constructive follow-up, EvoAudit turns these diagnostics into revisions of select, analyze, mutate, and gate, achieving the best performance.","thumbnail":"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2611.07624.png","numComments":1,"submittedBy":{"_id":"65e933fcacb29a596cd6eb6e","avatarUrl":"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65e933fcacb29a596cd6eb6e/dlH14rO2I4EUMVyjEjdRK.jpeg","fullname":"Xiaobo Wang","name":"Yofuria","type":"user","isPro":false,"isHf":false,"isHfAdmin":false,"isMod":false,"followerCount":6,"isUserFollowing":false},"organization":{"_id":"67ff908ff0f413c693b7cd0c","name":"ustc-community","fullname":"University of Science and Technology of China","avatar":"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/660c2d134ba2fcc848b03e21/j_f3uYYIFPH_4WJH9fKel.png"},"isAuthorParticipating":false},{"paper":{"id":"2611.06748","authors":[{"_id":"6aec3679870269964d13ae23","user":{"_id":"646d7f7bfe25e5f8d97666c5","avatarUrl":"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/noauth/HaC8y2l47WS9MC-Hw2rs6.jpeg","isPro":false,"fullname":"Yequan Bie","user":"Tommy-Bie","type":"user","name":"Tommy-Bie"},"name":"Yequan Bie","status":"claimed_verified","statusLastChangedAt":"2026-11-06T08:45:05.213Z","hidden":false},{"_id":"6aec3679870269964d13ae24","name":"Dengyang Jiang","hidden":false},{"_id":"6aec3679870269964d13ae25","name":"Zhixuan Chen","hidden":false},{"_id":"6aec3679870269964d13ae26","name":"Xiaozhuan Liang","hidden":false},{"_id":"6aec3679870269964d13ae27","name":"Haoxuan Che","hidden":false},{"_id":"6aec3679870269964d13ae28","name":"Quande Liu","hidden":false},{"_id":"6aec3679870269964d13ae29","name":"Xi Chen","hidden":false},{"_id":"6aec3679870269964d13ae2a","name":"Hao Chen","hidden":false}],"mediaUrls":["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/646d7f7bfe25e5f8d97666c5/GgxO9WDdVsi-livq39_SA.mp4"],"publishedAt":"2026-11-04T16:26:45.000Z","submittedOnDailyAt":"2026-11-09T00:00:00.000Z","title":"EvoWorld: Towards Out-of-Sight World Evolution in Generative Video Models","submittedOnDailyBy":{"_id":"646d7f7bfe25e5f8d97666c5","avatarUrl":"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/noauth/HaC8y2l47WS9MC-Hw2rs6.jpeg","isPro":false,"fullname":"Yequan Bie","user":"Tommy-Bie","type":"user","name":"Tommy-Bie"},"summary":"Generative video models are increasingly used as visual world simulators, yet their primary strength lies in rendering plausible pixels rather than reasoning about how a world should evolve. Coherent world evolution requires more than visual fidelity: when an event occurs, a model must infer its consequences for the entities in the world and then render the resulting state. For example, after a wild beast appears, the model should not only depict the wild beast, but also infer whether a nearby person should flee, take cover, or fight back based on the realized scene. Existing diffusion-based video models lack an explicit mechanism for maintaining world state and reasoning over such event-conditioned consequences, often producing visually plausible but behaviorally static or causally inconsistent outputs. To make this reasoning gap concrete and measurable, we propose a new problem, Out-of-Sight Causal Evolution, which requires a model to render an event, reason about its consequences for the evolving world state, and reveal a causally plausible outcome as the world continues to evolve, even out of sight. As a baseline reference solution, we introduce EvoWorld, a brain-renderer collaboration framework in which a vision-language model serves as the world brain: it observes generated segments, maintains an evolving account of the world, and reasons about plausible state transitions, while a segment-autoregressive video renderer realizes them visually. We further introduce EvoWorld-Data and EvoWorld-Bench for training and evaluating event realization, causal reactions, and revisit consistency. Experiments show that EvoWorld substantially improves event-conditioned causal evolution over recent models while preserving visual quality and consistency, establishing a reference point for reasoning-guided, autonomous world evolution beyond the current field of view.","upvotes":9,"discussionId":"6aec3679870269964d13ae2b","projectPage":"https://tommy-bie.github.io/projects/evoworld/","githubRepo":"https://github.com/Tommy-Bie/EvoWorld","githubRepoAddedBy":"user","githubStars":15},"publishedAt":"2026-11-04T11:26:45.000Z","title":"EvoWorld: Towards Out-of-Sight World Evolution in Generative Video Models","summary":"Generative video models are increasingly used as visual world simulators, yet their primary strength lies in rendering plausible pixels rather than reasoning about how a world should evolve. Coherent world evolution requires more than visual fidelity: when an event occurs, a model must infer its consequences for the entities in the world and then render the resulting state. For example, after a wild beast appears, the model should not only depict the wild beast, but also infer whether a nearby person should flee, take cover, or fight back based on the realized scene. Existing diffusion-based video models lack an explicit mechanism for maintaining world state and reasoning over such event-conditioned consequences, often producing visually plausible but behaviorally static or causally inconsistent outputs. To make this reasoning gap concrete and measurable, we propose a new problem, Out-of-Sight Causal Evolution, which requires a model to render an event, reason about its consequences for the evolving world state, and reveal a causally plausible outcome as the world continues to evolve, even out of sight. As a baseline reference solution, we introduce EvoWorld, a brain-renderer collaboration framework in which a vision-language model serves as the world brain: it observes generated segments, maintains an evolving account of the world, and reasons about plausible state transitions, while a segment-autoregressive video renderer realizes them visually. We further introduce EvoWorld-Data and EvoWorld-Bench for training and evaluating event realization, causal reactions, and revisit consistency. Experiments show that EvoWorld substantially improves event-conditioned causal evolution over recent models while preserving visual quality and consistency, establishing a reference point for reasoning-guided, autonomous world evolution beyond the current field of view.","mediaUrls":["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/646d7f7bfe25e5f8d97666c5/GgxO9WDdVsi-livq39_SA.mp4"],"thumbnail":"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2611.06748.png","numComments":1,"submittedBy":{"_id":"646d7f7bfe25e5f8d97666c5","avatarUrl":"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/noauth/HaC8y2l47WS9MC-Hw2rs6.jpeg","fullname":"Yequan Bie","name":"Tommy-Bie","type":"user","isPro":false,"isHf":false,"isHfAdmin":false,"isMod":false,"followerCount":1,"isUserFollowing":false},"isAuthorParticipating":true},{"paper":{"id":"2611.09894","authors":[{"_id":"6af148ba05af6789c0631efe","name":"Chaoyang Wang","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af148ba05af6789c0631eff","name":"Sicheng Gao","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af148ba05af6789c0631f00","name":"Junan Hu","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af148ba05af6789c0631f01","name":"Kunyang Li","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af148ba05af6789c0631f02","name":"Dongyang Chen","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af148ba05af6789c0631f03","name":"Yiyang Zhou","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af148ba05af6789c0631f04","name":"Huaxiu Yao","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af148ba05af6789c0631f05","name":"Yong Jae Lee","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af148ba05af6789c0631f06","name":"Radu Timofte","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af148ba05af6789c0631f07","name":"Yuzhang Shang","hidden":false}],"publishedAt":"2026-11-06T00:00:00.000Z","submittedOnDailyAt":"2026-11-09T00:00:00.000Z","title":"Video-Coder: Reinforcing Agentic Coding for Video Generation","submittedOnDailyBy":{"_id":"66ef7fdaad0dc3f0eb03d413","avatarUrl":"/avatars/6f8f34b5b1088cf0d4c7eb0bf4fae34d.svg","isPro":true,"fullname":"Chaoyang Wang","user":"ChaoyangWang","type":"user","name":"ChaoyangWang"},"summary":"Recent years have witnessed remarkable progress in video generation in terms of visual quality and temporal consistency. However, generating videos with precise spatial structure, coherent motion dynamics, and controllable scene composition remains challenging. In this paper, we introduce Video-Coder, the first attempt to train a code-driven video generation agent, which autonomously writes and executes code to construct a dynamic 3D scene (Draft Video) that provides structure and motion signals to downstream video generators. To support this paradigm, we design a dedicated data pipeline and curate two high-quality datasets, Video-Coder-SFT-6k and Video-Coder-RL-2k, which contain diverse prompts and ground-truth reference videos. We further develop the Video- Coder Harness, a unified interactive environment integrating code execution, 3D scene rendering, and execution feedback for agent training and inference. We then train Video-Coder with SFT fol- lowed by harness-native agentic reinforcement learning with dual reward feedback, which combines execution-based and video-based rewards. Additionally, we design an annealing training strategy that gradually reduces the execution reward over time, allowing the model to rely more on the video reward in the later training stages. Experiments show that Video-Coder achieves significant gains, improving Minimax-H3 by approximately 7 points on PhyGenBench and 24 points on VBench-2.0. We hope this work can serve as an open foundation for coding agents in video generation, and we fully open-source our data, models, and code","upvotes":4,"discussionId":"6af148bb05af6789c0631f08","projectPage":"https://cywang735.github.io/Video-Coder/","githubRepo":"https://github.com/CYWang735/Video-Coder","githubRepoAddedBy":"user","ai_summary":"Video-Coder is a code-driven video generation agent that writes and executes code to construct dynamic 3D scenes, trained with supervised fine-tuning and reinforcement learning using execution and video rewards to improve controllable video generation.","ai_keywords":["video generation","temporal consistency","spatial structure","motion dynamics","controllable scene composition","code-driven video generation agent","dynamic 3D scene","Draft Video","downstream video generators","supervised fine-tuning","reinforcement learning","harness-native agentic reinforcement learning","dual reward feedback","execution-based reward","video-based reward","annealing training strategy","3D scene rendering","execution feedback","Video-Coder Harness","PhyGenBench","VBench-2.0","Minimax-H3"],"ai_summary_model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash","organization":{"_id":"64f8a9c00590f3db14a59b8f","name":"UCF-Ai-LLMs","fullname":"University of Central Florida","avatar":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/6fb0661af6e4ec5ded3585562f94bbce?d=retro&size=100"}},"publishedAt":"2026-11-05T19:00:00.000Z","title":"Video-Coder: Reinforcing Agentic Coding for Video Generation","summary":"Recent years have witnessed remarkable progress in video generation in terms of visual quality and temporal consistency. However, generating videos with precise spatial structure, coherent motion dynamics, and controllable scene composition remains challenging. In this paper, we introduce Video-Coder, the first attempt to train a code-driven video generation agent, which autonomously writes and executes code to construct a dynamic 3D scene (Draft Video) that provides structure and motion signals to downstream video generators. To support this paradigm, we design a dedicated data pipeline and curate two high-quality datasets, Video-Coder-SFT-6k and Video-Coder-RL-2k, which contain diverse prompts and ground-truth reference videos. We further develop the Video- Coder Harness, a unified interactive environment integrating code execution, 3D scene rendering, and execution feedback for agent training and inference. We then train Video-Coder with SFT fol- lowed by harness-native agentic reinforcement learning with dual reward feedback, which combines execution-based and video-based rewards. Additionally, we design an annealing training strategy that gradually reduces the execution reward over time, allowing the model to rely more on the video reward in the later training stages. Experiments show that Video-Coder achieves significant gains, improving Minimax-H3 by approximately 7 points on PhyGenBench and 24 points on VBench-2.0. We hope this work can serve as an open foundation for coding agents in video generation, and we fully open-source our data, models, and code","thumbnail":"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2611.09894.png","numComments":1,"submittedBy":{"_id":"66ef7fdaad0dc3f0eb03d413","avatarUrl":"/avatars/6f8f34b5b1088cf0d4c7eb0bf4fae34d.svg","fullname":"Chaoyang Wang","name":"ChaoyangWang","type":"user","isPro":true,"isHf":false,"isHfAdmin":false,"isMod":false,"isUserFollowing":false},"organization":{"_id":"64f8a9c00590f3db14a59b8f","name":"UCF-Ai-LLMs","fullname":"University of Central Florida","avatar":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/6fb0661af6e4ec5ded3585562f94bbce?d=retro&size=100"},"isAuthorParticipating":false},{"paper":{"id":"2611.06043","authors":[{"_id":"6aec4ce8870269964d13aec5","user":{"_id":"6a9e88fa2bfa420de87da276","avatarUrl":"/avatars/ae76798364f2fdd80a683243ab9e7bf0.svg","isPro":false,"fullname":"Dani HG","user":"Hykaruu","type":"user","name":"Hykaruu"},"name":"Daniel Huang","status":"claimed_verified","statusLastChangedAt":"2026-11-05T11:33:39.709Z","hidden":false},{"_id":"6aec4ce8870269964d13aec6","name":"Qi He","hidden":false},{"_id":"6aec4ce8870269964d13aec7","name":"Shaoxiong Duan","hidden":false},{"_id":"6aec4ce8870269964d13aec8","name":"Yingjie Li","hidden":false},{"_id":"6aec4ce8870269964d13aec9","name":"Qiao Cheng","hidden":false},{"_id":"6aec4ce8870269964d13aeca","name":"Yitao Duan","hidden":false}],"mediaUrls":["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/6a9e88fa2bfa420de87da276/_VUVf7TSeDszMx2s84Bl-.qt"],"publishedAt":"2026-11-04T06:46:50.000Z","submittedOnDailyAt":"2026-11-09T00:00:00.000Z","title":"Confucius4-R2T2: A Stable-Prefix Streaming ASR Model for Real Real-Time Transcription","submittedOnDailyBy":{"_id":"6a9e88fa2bfa420de87da276","avatarUrl":"/avatars/ae76798364f2fdd80a683243ab9e7bf0.svg","isPro":false,"fullname":"Dani HG","user":"Hykaruu","type":"user","name":"Hykaruu"},"summary":"Streaming speech recognition is increasingly consumed by machines, such as translators and voice agents, that act on each token as it appears. A token that is later revised costs wasted work or a wrong action. In this work, we present Confucius4-R2T2 (short for Real Real-Time Transcription), a stable-prefix streaming ASR model that never modifies its earlier output while maintaining high accuracy and low latency. R2T2 is obtained by post-training Qwen3-ASR-1.7B on 1,500 hours of Chinese and English audio while freezing its pre-trained audio encoder and projector. The adaptation is based on the concept of Longest Stable Prefix (LSP), a new notion that we introduce in this work. We prove that LSP is monotone under the prefix order, consistent with the full-audio output, and maximal among labels that equal a whole earlier output. Learning from LSP labels converts a full-context model into a stable-prefix streaming one without any architectural change. Extensive evaluations show that R2T2 brings streaming recognition close to the base model's full-context quality while delivering stable text with low emission lag. Its gains extend beyond the post-training languages, demonstrating a practical path from full-context ASR to stable streaming. We open-source the model weights and code at https://huggingface.co/netease-youdao/Confucius4-R2T2 and https://github.com/netease-youdao/Confucius4-R2T2.","upvotes":4,"discussionId":"6aec4ce8870269964d13aecb","projectPage":"https://r2t2.ai/","githubRepo":"https://github.com/netease-youdao/Confucius4-R2T2","githubRepoAddedBy":"user","ai_summary":"A stable-prefix streaming speech recognition model achieves near full-context accuracy with low latency by post-training on longest stable prefix labels without architectural changes.","ai_keywords":["streaming ASR","stable-prefix","Longest Stable Prefix","monotone","prefix order","full-context model","Qwen3-ASR-1.7B"],"ai_summary_model":"thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small","githubStars":868,"organization":{"_id":"659841108982abaa5c6d03bb","name":"netease-youdao","fullname":"Netease Youdao","avatar":"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/63aa90473453852ef5458cd3/MT47gbYG6X_YnyQ2MR_DM.png"}},"publishedAt":"2026-11-04T01:46:50.000Z","title":"Confucius4-R2T2: A Stable-Prefix Streaming ASR Model for Real Real-Time Transcription","summary":"Streaming speech recognition is increasingly consumed by machines, such as translators and voice agents, that act on each token as it appears. A token that is later revised costs wasted work or a wrong action. In this work, we present Confucius4-R2T2 (short for Real Real-Time Transcription), a stable-prefix streaming ASR model that never modifies its earlier output while maintaining high accuracy and low latency. R2T2 is obtained by post-training Qwen3-ASR-1.7B on 1,500 hours of Chinese and English audio while freezing its pre-trained audio encoder and projector. The adaptation is based on the concept of Longest Stable Prefix (LSP), a new notion that we introduce in this work. We prove that LSP is monotone under the prefix order, consistent with the full-audio output, and maximal among labels that equal a whole earlier output. Learning from LSP labels converts a full-context model into a stable-prefix streaming one without any architectural change. Extensive evaluations show that R2T2 brings streaming recognition close to the base model's full-context quality while delivering stable text with low emission lag. Its gains extend beyond the post-training languages, demonstrating a practical path from full-context ASR to stable streaming. We open-source the model weights and code at https://huggingface.co/netease-youdao/Confucius4-R2T2 and https://github.com/netease-youdao/Confucius4-R2T2.","mediaUrls":["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/6a9e88fa2bfa420de87da276/_VUVf7TSeDszMx2s84Bl-.qt"],"thumbnail":"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2611.06043.png","numComments":1,"submittedBy":{"_id":"6a9e88fa2bfa420de87da276","avatarUrl":"/avatars/ae76798364f2fdd80a683243ab9e7bf0.svg","fullname":"Dani HG","name":"Hykaruu","type":"user","isPro":false,"isHf":false,"isHfAdmin":false,"isMod":false,"isUserFollowing":false},"organization":{"_id":"659841108982abaa5c6d03bb","name":"netease-youdao","fullname":"Netease Youdao","avatar":"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/63aa90473453852ef5458cd3/MT47gbYG6X_YnyQ2MR_DM.png"},"isAuthorParticipating":true},{"paper":{"id":"2610.35959","authors":[{"_id":"6af13d3d05af6789c0631ef2","name":"Junlong Li","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af13d3d05af6789c0631ef3","name":"Jindong Jiang","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af13d3d05af6789c0631ef4","name":"Yonggan Fu","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af13d3d05af6789c0631ef5","name":"Jiarui Yao","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af13d3d05af6789c0631ef6","name":"Hanrong Ye","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af13d3d05af6789c0631ef7","name":"Wonmin Byeon","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af13d3d05af6789c0631ef8","name":"Peter Belcak","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af13d3d05af6789c0631ef9","name":"Hao Zhang","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af13d3d05af6789c0631efa","name":"Pavlo Molchanov","hidden":false}],"publishedAt":"2026-10-25T00:00:00.000Z","submittedOnDailyAt":"2026-11-09T00:00:00.000Z","title":"AgentBaton: Efficient Model Collaboration in Agentic Tasks","submittedOnDailyBy":{"_id":"621e40ac944c7e36aaec2369","avatarUrl":"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/621e40ac944c7e36aaec2369/Yj-FJRWps3rvsS_B2bnKo.jpeg","isPro":false,"fullname":"Junlong Li","user":"lockon","type":"user","name":"lockon"},"summary":"Solving long-horizon agentic tasks requires language agents to interact with their environments over multiple steps, and reliable performance often comes from the expensive capable models available through cloud providers. Meanwhile, alternative efficient models with inferior performance but a much lower cost are emerging, including lower-tier cloud models and smaller open-weights models deployable on local hardware. This raises a natural question that remains less explored than traditional query-level routing: can capable and efficient models collaborate within a single long-horizon agentic task to substantially reduce cost while preserving task accuracy? In this paper, we systematically investigate key design choices for such collaboration through model handoffs, where one model takes over from another, focusing on handoff direction and context transfer. We find that efficient-to-capable handoffs generally underperform capable-only execution with limited or even no cost reduction, whereas capable-to-efficient handoffs at suitable stages of task progress largely preserve or even improve accuracy while substantially reducing cost. These findings suggest that a one-time, simple, well-timed handoff strategy is good enough for cost-effective agent collaboration, motivating AgentBaton, a lightweight detector that learns when to hand off from recurring patterns in task progress. Across 11 model pairs and two representative benchmarks, AgentBaton reduces API costs by 65.9% on average while largely preserving task accuracy, with accuracy gains of up to 8.8 percentage points over capable-only execution. Experiments across various reasoning-effort settings further show that AgentBaton extends the empirical accuracy-cost Pareto frontier.","upvotes":3,"discussionId":"6af13d3e05af6789c0631efb","ai_summary":"Capable-to-efficient model handoffs at suitable task stages preserve accuracy while cutting costs, and a lightweight detector, AgentBaton, achieves large API cost savings and extends the accuracy-cost Pareto frontier.","ai_keywords":["language agents","long-horizon agentic tasks","model handoffs","handoff direction","context transfer","efficient-to-capable handoffs","capable-to-efficient handoffs","query-level routing","open-weights models","reasoning-effort settings","accuracy-cost Pareto frontier","AgentBaton","lightweight detector"],"ai_summary_model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash","organization":{"_id":"60262b67268c201cdc8b7d43","name":"nvidia","fullname":"NVIDIA","avatar":"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65df9200dc3292a8983e5017/Vs5FPVCH-VZBipV3qKTuy.png"}},"publishedAt":"2026-10-24T20:00:00.000Z","title":"AgentBaton: Efficient Model Collaboration in Agentic Tasks","summary":"Solving long-horizon agentic tasks requires language agents to interact with their environments over multiple steps, and reliable performance often comes from the expensive capable models available through cloud providers. Meanwhile, alternative efficient models with inferior performance but a much lower cost are emerging, including lower-tier cloud models and smaller open-weights models deployable on local hardware. This raises a natural question that remains less explored than traditional query-level routing: can capable and efficient models collaborate within a single long-horizon agentic task to substantially reduce cost while preserving task accuracy? In this paper, we systematically investigate key design choices for such collaboration through model handoffs, where one model takes over from another, focusing on handoff direction and context transfer. We find that efficient-to-capable handoffs generally underperform capable-only execution with limited or even no cost reduction, whereas capable-to-efficient handoffs at suitable stages of task progress largely preserve or even improve accuracy while substantially reducing cost. These findings suggest that a one-time, simple, well-timed handoff strategy is good enough for cost-effective agent collaboration, motivating AgentBaton, a lightweight detector that learns when to hand off from recurring patterns in task progress. Across 11 model pairs and two representative benchmarks, AgentBaton reduces API costs by 65.9% on average while largely preserving task accuracy, with accuracy gains of up to 8.8 percentage points over capable-only execution. Experiments across various reasoning-effort settings further show that AgentBaton extends the empirical accuracy-cost Pareto frontier.","thumbnail":"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.35959.png","numComments":1,"submittedBy":{"_id":"621e40ac944c7e36aaec2369","avatarUrl":"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/621e40ac944c7e36aaec2369/Yj-FJRWps3rvsS_B2bnKo.jpeg","fullname":"Junlong Li","name":"lockon","type":"user","isPro":false,"isHf":false,"isHfAdmin":false,"isMod":false,"followerCount":11,"isUserFollowing":false},"organization":{"_id":"60262b67268c201cdc8b7d43","name":"nvidia","fullname":"NVIDIA","avatar":"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65df9200dc3292a8983e5017/Vs5FPVCH-VZBipV3qKTuy.png"},"isAuthorParticipating":false},{"paper":{"id":"2611.09382","authors":[{"_id":"6af13a6605af6789c0631ee7","name":"Junru Hu","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af13a6605af6789c0631ee8","name":"Cheng Tang","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af13a6605af6789c0631ee9","name":"Wei Li","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af13a6605af6789c0631eea","name":"Hongze Zhu","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af13a6605af6789c0631eeb","name":"Chun-Mei Feng","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af13a6605af6789c0631eec","name":"Qi Zhang","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af13a6605af6789c0631eed","name":"Yirong Chen","hidden":false},{"_id":"6af13a6605af6789c0631eee","name":"Ming Hu","hidden":false}],"publishedAt":"2026-11-06T00:00:00.000Z","submittedOnDailyAt":"2026-11-09T00:00:00.000Z","title":"Exploring Weight-Space Programming: Capabilities, Generalization, and Limitations","submittedOnDailyBy":{"_id":"625964cfac7b9f4ddcc19bb3","avatarUrl":"/avatars/f5ae61d34ef0e80dc46b49097167f90a.svg","isPro":false,"fullname":"Ming Hu","user":"xioamiyh","type":"user","name":"xioamiyh"},"summary":"Weight-Space Programming (WSP) enables reasoning agents to directly inspect and modify neural network parameters, offering an alternative to gradient-based model adaptation. However, its capabilities, cross-task generalization, and practical limitations remain insufficiently understood. We systematically investigate WSP through 16 editing trajectories across seven benchmarks and two model scales (Qwen3.5-0.8B and Qwen3.5-9B). Our findings reveal three key insights. First, WSP can achieve substantial performance gains through highly localized interventions. On full held-out evaluations, accuracy improves by up to 29.45, 4.42, 36.72, and 17.11 percentage points on MATH-500, GPQA Diamond, IFEval, and SciBench, respectively. Notably, modifying only 1,023 parameters yields a 29.45-point improvement. Second, WSP exhibits promising but uneven cross-task generalization. A Math-edited checkpoint improves GPQA Diamond, IFEval, and SciBench by 3.03, 12.18, and 20.10 points, respectively, without further editing, while leaving SciCode performance unchanged. Third, effective editing remains challenging and inconsistent. Outcomes vary substantially across trajectories, and six of the 16 trajectories retain the original weights when candidate improvements cannot be reliably validated. Together, these results highlight WSP's potential for highly localized model adaptation while revealing important challenges in intervention discovery, reliability, and cross-task transfer.\n  Homepage: https://wsp-scope.github.io/ GitHub: https://github.com/WSP-Scope/WSP-Scope-Project","upvotes":3,"discussionId":"6af13a6705af6789c0631eef","projectPage":"https://wsp-scope.github.io/","githubRepo":"https://github.com/WSP-Scope/WSP-Scope-Project","githubRepoAddedBy":"user","ai_summary":"Weight-Space Programming can improve reasoning performance through small, localized edits to neural network parameters, but its editing reliability and cross-task generalization remain uneven.","ai_keywords":["Weight-Space Programming","WSP","reasoning agents","neural network parameters","gradient-based model adaptation","editing trajectories","cross-task generalization","localized interventions","held-out evaluations","checkpoints","model weights","intervention discovery","cross-task transfer","Qwen3.5-0.8B","Qwen3.5-9B","MATH-500","GPQA Diamond","IFEval","SciBench","SciCode"],"ai_summary_model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash","githubStars":1},"publishedAt":"2026-11-05T19:00:00.000Z","title":"Exploring Weight-Space Programming: Capabilities, Generalization, and Limitations","summary":"Weight-Space Programming (WSP) enables reasoning agents to directly inspect and modify neural network parameters, offering an alternative to gradient-based model adaptation. However, its capabilities, cross-task generalization, and practical limitations remain insufficiently understood. We systematically investigate WSP through 16 editing trajectories across seven benchmarks and two model scales (Qwen3.5-0.8B and Qwen3.5-9B). Our findings reveal three key insights. First, WSP can achieve substantial performance gains through highly localized interventions. On full held-out evaluations, accuracy improves by up to 29.45, 4.42, 36.72, and 17.11 percentage points on MATH-500, GPQA Diamond, IFEval, and SciBench, respectively. Notably, modifying only 1,023 parameters yields a 29.45-point improvement. Second, WSP exhibits promising but uneven cross-task generalization. A Math-edited checkpoint improves GPQA Diamond, IFEval, and SciBench by 3.03, 12.18, and 20.10 points, respectively, without further editing, while leaving SciCode performance unchanged. Third, effective editing remains challenging and inconsistent. Outcomes vary substantially across trajectories, and six of the 16 trajectories retain the original weights when candidate improvements cannot be reliably validated. Together, these results highlight WSP's potential for highly localized model adaptation while revealing important challenges in intervention discovery, reliability, and cross-task transfer.\n  Homepage: https://wsp-scope.github.io/ GitHub: https://github.com/WSP-Scope/WSP-Scope-Project","thumbnail":"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2611.09382.png","numComments":1,"submittedBy":{"_id":"625964cfac7b9f4ddcc19bb3","avatarUrl":"/avatars/f5ae61d34ef0e80dc46b49097167f90a.svg","fullname":"Ming Hu","name":"xioamiyh","type":"user","isPro":false,"isHf":false,"isHfAdmin":false,"isMod":false,"followerCount":2,"isUserFollowing":false},"isAuthorParticipating":false},{"paper":{"id":"2611.07461","authors":[{"_id":"6aed40950962bbebb014f2d1","name":"Haibo Jin","hidden":false},{"_id":"6aed40950962bbebb014f2d2","name":"Peng Kuang","hidden":false},{"_id":"6aed40950962bbebb014f2d3","name":"Xiaopeng Yuan","hidden":false},{"_id":"6aed40950962bbebb014f2d4","name":"Lucheng Fu","hidden":false},{"_id":"6aed40950962bbebb014f2d5","name":"Jerry Wang","hidden":false},{"_id":"6aed40950962bbebb014f2d6","name":"Xucheng Yu","hidden":false},{"_id":"6aed40950962bbebb014f2d7","user":{"_id":"68693c32c7795984acd66feb","avatarUrl":"/avatars/3b17ca9d6043ae78e31a1a79548b4eec.svg","isPro":false,"fullname":"YaoningYu","user":"YaoningYu","type":"user","name":"YaoningYu"},"name":"Yaoning Yu","status":"claimed_verified","statusLastChangedAt":"2026-11-06T08:45:05.257Z","hidden":false},{"_id":"6aed40950962bbebb014f2d8","name":"Dehao Wu","hidden":false},{"_id":"6aed40950962bbebb014f2d9","name":"Runguo Li","hidden":false},{"_id":"6aed40950962bbebb014f2da","name":"Colin Su","hidden":false},{"_id":"6aed40950962bbebb014f2db","name":"Jingxuan Sun","hidden":false},{"_id":"6aed40950962bbebb014f2dc","name":"Xuyang Liu","hidden":false},{"_id":"6aed40950962bbebb014f2dd","name":"Haojing Luo","hidden":false},{"_id":"6aed40950962bbebb014f2de","user":{"_id":"670d920206218f52e8ea376d","avatarUrl":"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/no-auth/j_-zv1ftLCJbjjw9-wnN8.png","isPro":false,"fullname":"Haohan Wang","user":"haohanw","type":"user","name":"haohanw"},"name":"Haohan Wang","status":"claimed_verified","statusLastChangedAt":"2026-11-08T08:45:04.361Z","hidden":false}],"mediaUrls":["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/65085d39fec2f376356e9ba5/l_yHc-FFvhYpWxasNu_aQ.png"],"publishedAt":"2026-11-05T00:00:00.000Z","submittedOnDailyAt":"2026-11-09T00:00:00.000Z","title":"The Last Mile to the Real World: Agent-Native Primitives as Reusable Building Blocks for Solving Real-World Tasks","submittedOnDailyBy":{"_id":"65085d39fec2f376356e9ba5","avatarUrl":"/avatars/43be8b5dfbe067b9ccd519d8c4b6514f.svg","isPro":false,"fullname":"Haibo Jin","user":"AllenJin","type":"user","name":"AllenJin"},"summary":"How can an agent handle a real-world task end to end? Frontier models with their own harnesses still fall short on real-world benchmarks: on Terminal-Bench 4.0, GPT-5.6 Sol with Codex resolves 37.3% of the tasks and Claude Opus 5 with Claude Code 51.8%. These tasks fail at the step from reaching a resource to applying it, which we call the last mile to the real world. Existing approaches put the resource within reach but leave the competence to apply it with the caller, so each harness rebuilds that competence. We introduce agent-native primitives, reusable units that equip a resource with a resident LLM that applies it in context and communicates with other primitives, and HEELO (Harness Engineering around Evolving Agent-Native Primitives for the Last Mile to Real-WOrld Tasks), a harness engineering framework that builds each task's harness from a library of such primitives. HEELO composes the primitives a task needs into a task-specific graph, verifies the result against evidence from the environment, revises the primitives a failure implicates, and admits new or improved primitives after validation. We instantiate HEELO with eight primitive families, each built for one kind of task, and evaluate them on more than twenty benchmarks. Dev-Primitives raise Terminal-Bench 4.0 resolution from 37.3% under Codex to 55.5% on the same backbone, Harness Primitives composed per task raise Terminal-Bench 2.0 Pass@1 from 60.0% for the seed harness to 72.2%, Tool Primitives raise ToolBench pass rate from 55.8% to 75.1%, and Navigation Primitives raise GAIA-Text-103 accuracy from 45.6% to 58.3%. On the 46 real-world tasks of our heelo.dev platform, Claude Opus 5.5 with Claude Code and GPT-6.1 Sol with Codex complete 9 each on their own and 39 and 35 when connected to HEELO through our MCP server, with 1.9times and 2.3times less time and 1.55times lower cost per completed task.","upvotes":0,"discussionId":"6aed40950962bbebb014f2df","projectPage":"https://www.heelo.dev/"},"publishedAt":"2026-11-04T19:00:00.000Z","title":"The Last Mile to the Real World: Agent-Native Primitives as Reusable Building Blocks for Solving Real-World Tasks","summary":"How can an agent handle a real-world task end to end? Frontier models with their own harnesses still fall short on real-world benchmarks: on Terminal-Bench 4.0, GPT-5.6 Sol with Codex resolves 37.3% of the tasks and Claude Opus 5 with Claude Code 51.8%. These tasks fail at the step from reaching a resource to applying it, which we call the last mile to the real world. Existing approaches put the resource within reach but leave the competence to apply it with the caller, so each harness rebuilds that competence. We introduce agent-native primitives, reusable units that equip a resource with a resident LLM that applies it in context and communicates with other primitives, and HEELO (Harness Engineering around Evolving Agent-Native Primitives for the Last Mile to Real-WOrld Tasks), a harness engineering framework that builds each task's harness from a library of such primitives. HEELO composes the primitives a task needs into a task-specific graph, verifies the result against evidence from the environment, revises the primitives a failure implicates, and admits new or improved primitives after validation. We instantiate HEELO with eight primitive families, each built for one kind of task, and evaluate them on more than twenty benchmarks. Dev-Primitives raise Terminal-Bench 4.0 resolution from 37.3% under Codex to 55.5% on the same backbone, Harness Primitives composed per task raise Terminal-Bench 2.0 Pass@1 from 60.0% for the seed harness to 72.2%, Tool Primitives raise ToolBench pass rate from 55.8% to 75.1%, and Navigation Primitives raise GAIA-Text-103 accuracy from 45.6% to 58.3%. On the 46 real-world tasks of our heelo.dev platform, Claude Opus 5.5 with Claude Code and GPT-6.1 Sol with Codex complete 9 each on their own and 39 and 35 when connected to HEELO through our MCP server, with 1.9times and 2.3times less time and 1.55times lower cost per completed task.","mediaUrls":["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/65085d39fec2f376356e9ba5/l_yHc-FFvhYpWxasNu_aQ.png"],"thumbnail":"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2611.07461.png","numComments":1,"submittedBy":{"_id":"65085d39fec2f376356e9ba5","avatarUrl":"/avatars/43be8b5dfbe067b9ccd519d8c4b6514f.svg","fullname":"Haibo Jin","name":"AllenJin","type":"user","isPro":false,"isHf":false,"isHfAdmin":false,"isMod":false,"isUserFollowing":false},"isAuthorParticipating":true},{"paper":{"id":"2611.04434","authors":[{"_id":"6aefd49005af6789c0631ac5","user":{"_id":"6317b77f83d8d2fd90326858","avatarUrl":"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/6317b77f83d8d2fd90326858/b9CZJAyAFskk5NRkbHZAB.jpeg","isPro":false,"fullname":"Neo Mohsenvand","user":"mohsenvand","type":"user","name":"mohsenvand"},"name":"Mostafa 'Neo' Mohsenvand","status":"claimed_verified","statusLastChangedAt":"2026-11-08T08:45:04.368Z","hidden":false}],"mediaUrls":["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/6317b77f83d8d2fd90326858/QsmBIj5Deb_s58RwOZmPL.gif"],"publishedAt":"2026-11-03T07:25:42.000Z","submittedOnDailyAt":"2026-11-09T00:00:00.000Z","title":"Sketchpad: certified exact arithmetic with a small vision-language model","submittedOnDailyBy":{"_id":"6317b77f83d8d2fd90326858","avatarUrl":"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/6317b77f83d8d2fd90326858/b9CZJAyAFskk5NRkbHZAB.jpeg","isPro":false,"fullname":"Neo Mohsenvand","user":"mohsenvand","type":"user","name":"mohsenvand"},"summary":"Language models are accurate on most long-arithmetic problems, but their accuracy is estimated on samples and carries no guarantee for an unseen input; tool use is exact but moves the computation outside the network. We show that exactness at all input lengths becomes a finite, checkable property of a network when each of its steps reads only a bounded view of an external working memory. In Sketchpad, Qwen3.5-0.8B with a low-rank adapter executes arithmetic procedures on a rendered sheet. Each forward pass reads a three-cell view and emits one step: a digit, a carry or borrow, and the next operation. A fixed runtime moves the view and writes no digit of a result. We prove in Lean that the model is then exact on all inputs of any length if and only if it is exact on the finite set of views the runtime presents, that no finite test suffices when steps also read earlier steps, and that the guarantee tolerates bounded numerical perturbation and extends to compositions of procedures. All 2,446 views pass on the deployment device, a laptop GPU running the model in a web browser. The certified model performs signed addition and subtraction, long multiplication and division, square roots and greatest common divisors at arbitrary length. It solved 200 of 200 pre-registered problems in the browser, and 220 of 220 sums of up to 1,000 digits and products of up to 20 digits, of which GPT-6.1 Sol, the strongest of ten other models, solved 217. A symbolic runtime built on these procedures solves, expands and factors polynomials with every number computed by the model, and a multiplicative gate on the adapter recovers the base model exactly when the sheet is not in use.","upvotes":0,"discussionId":"6aefd49105af6789c0631ac6","projectPage":"https://neovand.github.io/sketchpad/","githubRepo":"https://github.com/NeoVand/sketchpad","githubRepoAddedBy":"user","githubStars":0},"publishedAt":"2026-11-03T02:25:42.000Z","title":"Sketchpad: certified exact arithmetic with a small vision-language model","summary":"Language models are accurate on most long-arithmetic problems, but their accuracy is estimated on samples and carries no guarantee for an unseen input; tool use is exact but moves the computation outside the network. We show that exactness at all input lengths becomes a finite, checkable property of a network when each of its steps reads only a bounded view of an external working memory. In Sketchpad, Qwen3.5-0.8B with a low-rank adapter executes arithmetic procedures on a rendered sheet. Each forward pass reads a three-cell view and emits one step: a digit, a carry or borrow, and the next operation. A fixed runtime moves the view and writes no digit of a result. We prove in Lean that the model is then exact on all inputs of any length if and only if it is exact on the finite set of views the runtime presents, that no finite test suffices when steps also read earlier steps, and that the guarantee tolerates bounded numerical perturbation and extends to compositions of procedures. All 2,446 views pass on the deployment device, a laptop GPU running the model in a web browser. The certified model performs signed addition and subtraction, long multiplication and division, square roots and greatest common divisors at arbitrary length. It solved 200 of 200 pre-registered problems in the browser, and 220 of 220 sums of up to 1,000 digits and products of up to 20 digits, of which GPT-6.1 Sol, the strongest of ten other models, solved 217. A symbolic runtime built on these procedures solves, expands and factors polynomials with every number computed by the model, and a multiplicative gate on the adapter recovers the base model exactly when the sheet is not in use.","mediaUrls":["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/6317b77f83d8d2fd90326858/QsmBIj5Deb_s58RwOZmPL.gif"],"thumbnail":"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2611.04434.png","numComments":1,"submittedBy":{"_id":"6317b77f83d8d2fd90326858","avatarUrl":"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/6317b77f83d8d2fd90326858/b9CZJAyAFskk5NRkbHZAB.jpeg","fullname":"Neo Mohsenvand","name":"mohsenvand","type":"user","isPro":false,"isHf":false,"isHfAdmin":false,"isMod":false,"followerCount":1,"isUserFollowing":false},"isAuthorParticipating":true}]